 ...)` works. I'll use the string form or setattr(socket, 'gethostbyname', ...).\n\nNow, one more consideration: the `if ipaddr is None` branch requires `self._proxy.remote_dns` truthy AND inet_aton failure. Test 2 covers it.\n\nAlso the implicit else of the atype chain (unknown atype \u2192 destaddr unchanged): lines 243-249 \u2014 if atype is something else (e.g., 0), destaddr remains the encoded/original value... wait, in the None-ipaddr case destaddr was reassigned to encoded bytes at line 226. In the IPv4-request case, destaddr remains the original string. Then `return (destaddr, destport)`. An unknown atype test would cover the fall-through. Is that a \"branch\"? The elif chain \u2014 covering all three elifs plus the implicit else. Coverage tools (branch coverage) would count the implicit else of the if/elif chain? For `if/elif/elif` without else, branch coverage measures each condition's true/false outcomes. The last `elif atype == ATYP_IPV6:` false outcome leads to the code after the chain (line 250). So to get full branch coverage, we need a case where atype is none of the three \u2192 falls to line 250 with destaddr unchanged. Hmm, does coverage.py count that? For `if A: ... elif B: ... elif C: ...` \u2014 branch coverage tracks arcs: from the if-line to each branch target and to the fall-through. The arc from line 243 (if) to line 250 (fall-through when all false) \u2014 yes, coverage.py would want that arc covered. So I should add a test with an unknown atype (e.g., atype=0 or 2). Let me add test 7: response atype = 0 (invalid) \u2192 destaddr returned unchanged (the original string '1.2.3.4'), destport parsed. recv calls: [2, 4, 2] (no addr read). Result: ('1.2.3.4', port).\n\nHmm wait, in that case with ipaddr not None (IP literal), destaddr is still the original string '1.2.3.4'. Return ('1.2.3.4', 4242). Good \u2014 that covers the fall-through arc.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: is that arc real in coverage.py? The statement lines 243-249: line 243 `if atype == ...:`, 244 body, 245 `elif`, 246-247 body, 248 `elif`, 249 body, 250 `destport = ...`. Arcs: 243\u2192244 (true), 243\u2192245 (false), 245\u2192246 (true), 245\u2192248 (false), 248\u2192249 (true), 248\u2192250 (false). So yes, to cover arc 248\u2192250 we need atype not in (1,3,4). Test 7 handles it. \n\nNow let me also double-check the `if ipaddr is None` / else arcs: line 225 `if ipaddr is None:` \u2192 226 (true) / 230 (false). Tests 2 (true) and 1 (false). Good.\n\n`if status != ERR_SUCCESS:` line 239 \u2192 240 (true, test 4) / 243 (false, tests 1,2,3,5?,6,7). Test 5 raises at 237 before reaching 239. Fine.\n\nNow, writing the recv side_effect lists carefully.\n\nTest 1 (IPv4 request, IPv4 response):\n- recv(2) \u2192 b'\\x05\\x00' (auth: version 5, method AUTH_NONE=0)\n- recv(4) \u2192 b'\\x05\\x00\\x00\\x01' (version 5, status 0, reserved 0, atype 1)\n- recv(4) \u2192 b'\\x0a\\x0b\\x0c\\x0d' (bound addr 10.11.12.13)\n- recv(2) \u2192 b'\\x10\\x92' (port 4242 = 0x1092)\n\nResult: (b'\\x0a\\x0b\\x0c\\x0d', 4242).\n\nLet me use compat_struct_pack for building: \n- auth = compat_struct_pack('!BB', SOCKS5_VERSION, Socks5Auth.AUTH_NONE)\n- header = compat_struct_pack('!BBBB', SOCKS5_VERSION, 0, 0, Socks5AddressType.ATYP_IPV4)\n- addr = compat_struct_pack('!BBBB', 10, 11, 12, 13)\n- port = compat_struct_pack('!H', 4242)\n\nTest 2 (domain request via remote DNS, domainname response):\ndestaddr='example.com', port=1080.\n- recv(2) \u2192 auth b'\\x05\\x00'\n- recv(4) \u2192 header with atype=ATYP_DOMAINNAME: b'\\x05\\x00\\x00\\x03'\n- recv(1) \u2192 b'\\x0b' (alen=11)\n- recv(11) \u2192 b'example.org'\n- recv(2) \u2192 b'\\x04\\x38' (port 1080 = 0x0438)\n\nResult: (b'example.org', 1080).\n\nRequest packet assertion: connect_packet == compat_struct_pack('!BBB', SOCKS5_VERSION, Socks5Command.CMD_CONNECT, 0) + compat_struct_pack('!B', Socks5AddressType.ATYP_DOMAINNAME) + compat_struct_pack('!B', 11) + b'example.com' + compat_struct_pack('!H', 1080).\n\nTest 3 (IPv6 response):\ndestaddr='1.2.3.4' (IP literal \u2192 IPv4 request), response atype=ATYP_IPV6.\n- recv(2) \u2192 auth\n- recv(4) \u2192 b'\\x05\\x00\\x00\\x04'\n- recv(16) \u2192 16 bytes, e.g., compat_struct_pack('!4H', 0x2001, 0xdb8, 0, 1) \u2192 wait that's 8 bytes. '!8H' \u2192 16 bytes: compat_struct_pack('!8H', 0x2001, 0x0db8, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1).\n- recv(2) \u2192 port 4242.\n\nResult: (those 16 bytes, 4242).\n\nTest 4 (error):\n- recv(2) \u2192 auth\n- recv(4) \u2192 b'\\x05\\x01\\x00\\x00' (status=1)\nThen raises. recv calls: [2, 4]. close called once. send called twice.\n\nTest 5 (bad version):\n- recv(2) \u2192 auth\n- recv(4) \u2192 b'\\x04\\x00\\x00\\x00' (version 4)\nRaises InvalidVersionError. close called once.\n\nTest 6 (rdns=False, domain resolved locally):\ndestaddr='example.com', rdns=False. Mock socket.gethostbyname \u2192 '9.9.9.9'.\n- recv(2) \u2192 auth\n- recv(4) \u2192 b'\\x05\\x00\\x00\\x01'\n- recv(4) \u2192 b'\\x01\\x02\\x03\\x04'\n- recv(2) \u2192 b'\\x1f\\x90' (8080 = 0x1F90)\n\nResult: (b'\\x01\\x02\\x03\\x04', 8080). connect_packet contains ATYP_IPV4 + b'\\x09\\x09\\x09\\x09'.\n\nTest 7 (unknown atype fall-through):\ndestaddr='1.2.3.4', response atype=0.\n- recv(2) \u2192 auth\n- recv(4) \u2192 b'\\x05\\x00\\x00\\x00' (atype 0)\n- recv(2) \u2192 port\nResult: ('1.2.3.4', port). recv calls [2, 4, 2].\n\nNow, the auth method response: `_socks5_auth` checks `if method == Socks5Auth.AUTH_NO_ACCEPTABLE or (method == AUTH_USER_PASS and ...)`. With method=AUTH_NONE (0), proceeds. Good. I use `Socks5Auth.AUTH_NONE` symbolically.\n\nAlso `_socks5_auth` sends auth packet: version + count + methods. With username/password None: methods = [AUTH_NONE], packet = b'\\x05\\x01\\x00'. I'll assert that in test 1.\n\nNow the fixture and cleanup. Let me write:\n\n```python\n@pytest.fixture\ndef socks():\n    sock = sockssocket()\n    yield sock\n    # Ensure the underlying real socket is closed even if `close` was mocked.\n    socket.socket.close(sock)\n```\n\nHmm \u2014 `socket.socket.close(sock)`: `socket.socket` is the class; `.close` unbound method; passing sock. Works. But wait \u2014 if monkeypatch already restored and sock was never closed, this closes it. If sock.close() was called for real somewhere (not in our tests since we mock it), double close is fine.\n\nBut hold on \u2014 there's a potential issue with fixture teardown ordering vs monkeypatch. As discussed, `socket.socket.close(sock)` bypasses any instance attribute, so ordering doesn't matter. \n\nActually, wait: is there an issue calling `socket.socket.close(sock)` if `sock`'s fd is -1 or already closed? `socket.close()` on closed socket: in Python 3, `socket.close()` is safe to call multiple times (it checks `if self._closed`... actually the internal `_io` handling). Yes, idempotent.\n\nAlternatively, I can make the fixture take monkeypatch... no, keep it independent.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider whether to use monkeypatch.setattr on the instance vs. just direct assignment with cleanup. monkeypatch is cleaner and the user suggested it. `monkeypatch.setattr(sock, 'recv', recv_mock)` \u2014 monkeypatch.setattr with an object and name: it checks the attribute exists (it does, on the class), sets instance attr, and on teardown deletes the instance attr (since it didn't exist on the instance before \u2014 monkeypatch records it as \"not in instance dict\" and deletes). Actually, how does monkeypatch handle instance attributes that shadow class attributes? `monkeypatch.setattr(obj, name, value)` uses `setattr(obj, name, value)` and records the old value via `getattr(obj, name)`... Hmm, the undo does `setattr(obj, name, oldval)` which would set the bound method as an instance attribute \u2014 slightly messy but functionally equivalent. Actually, pytest's monkeypatch: `monkeypatch.setattr(target, name, value)` \u2014 it stores `(target, name, oldval)` where oldval = getattr(target, name) (the bound method), and undo does setattr(target, name, oldval) \u2014 setting the bound method as instance attribute. That leaves the instance with a bound-method instance attribute after teardown, but our fixture closes the socket via the class anyway, and the socket is discarded. No state pollution beyond the discarded object. Fine.\n\nHmm wait, actually there's a subtlety: pytest monkeypatch for instance attributes \u2014 there's `monkeypatch.setattr(obj, name, value, raising=True)`. It uses `inspect.getmodule`... no. It just does setattr and records. On undo: `setattr(obj, name, old)`. OK fine.\n\nAlternatively, use `mock.patch.object(sock, 'recv', side_effect=[...])` as context manager. But monkeypatch is fine and suggested.\n\nLet me now also think: do we need `sock.setproxy` or can we set `_proxy` directly? `setproxy` asserts proxytype in (ProxyType.SOCKS4, ProxyType.SOCKS4A, ProxyType.SOCKS5). Using ProxyType.SOCKS5 attribute \u2014 safe. And Proxy construction: `Proxy(proxytype, addr, port, username, password, rdns)`. I don't know Proxy's exact definition (namedtuple with 6 fields presumably, with rdns stored maybe as 'remote_dns'). Since `_resolve_address` accesses `self._proxy.remote_dns`, Proxy must expose `remote_dns`. setproxy maps rdns \u2192 remote_dns presumably. Using setproxy is the safe path. \n\nOne more check \u2014 `_socks5_auth` with username=None, password=None: `if self._proxy.username and self._proxy.password:` \u2192 False \u2192 only AUTH_NONE offered. Server responds AUTH_NONE \u2192 OK. Good, no user/pass sub-exchange.\n\nNow, imports. From youtube_dl.socks: sockssocket, ProxyType, Socks5Auth, Socks5AddressType, Socks5Command, Socks5Error, InvalidVersionError, SOCKS5_VERSION. From youtube_dl.compat: compat_struct_pack, compat_struct_unpack (maybe not needed \u2014 for asserting result port I can just compare ints; for building responses I need pack). compat_ord not needed in tests.\n\nWait \u2014 is `Socks5Auth` exported? It's defined in socks.py. Yes.\n\nIs `InvalidVersionError` defined in socks.py? `_check_response_version` references it directly, so yes.\n\nLet me also double check `Socks5Command.CMD_CONNECT` \u2014 from Socks4Command: CMD_CONNECT = 1. Good.\n\nNow, about `mock` import: `from unittest import mock`.\n\nLet me write the helper for building the socket and mocking:\n\n```python\ndef _make_socket(monkeypatch, rdns=True, recv_chunks=(), username=None, password=None):\n    sock = sockssocket()\n    sock.setproxy(ProxyType.SOCKS5, '127.0.0.1', 1080, rdns=rdns, username=username, password=password)\n    recv_mock = mock.Mock(side_effect=list(recv_chunks))\n    send_mock = mock.Mock()\n    close_mock = mock.Mock()\n    monkeypatch.setattr(sock, 'recv', recv_mock)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(sock, 'sendall', send_mock)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(sock, 'close', close_mock)\n    return sock, recv_mock, send_mock, close_mock\n```\n\nHmm, but the fixture already creates the socket. Let me restructure: fixture `socks` creates and cleans up; helper takes the sock and monkeypatch and sets mocks. Or simpler: each test creates its own socket via a helper that also registers cleanup via the fixture... Let me just do:\n\n```python\n@pytest.fixture\ndef socks():\n    sock = sockssocket()\n    yield sock\n    socket.socket.close(sock)\n```\n\nAnd a helper:\n\n```python\ndef _patch_io(monkeypatch, sock, recv_chunks):\n    recv_mock = mock.Mock(side_effect=list(recv_chunks))\n    send_mock = mock.Mock()\n    close_mock = mock.Mock()\n    monkeypatch.setattr(sock, 'recv', recv_mock)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(sock, 'sendall', send_mock)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(sock, 'close', close_mock)\n    return recv_mock, send_mock, close_mock\n```\n\nAnd a helper for auth reply:\n\n```python\nAUTH_REPLY = compat_struct_pack('!BB', SOCKS5_VERSION, Socks5Auth.AUTH_NONE)\n```\n\nWait \u2014 careful: `_recv_bytes(2)` does `compat_struct_unpack('!2B', data)` \u2014 needs exactly 2 bytes. b'\\x05\\x00' \u2014 good.\n\nNow write tests:\n\n```python\nclass TestSocks5Setup:\n    def test_connect_ip_literal_ipv4_reply(self, socks, monkeypatch):\n        bound_addr = compat_struct_pack('!BBBB', 10, 11, 12, 13)\n        recv_mock, send_mock, close_mock = _patch_io(monkeypatch, socks, [\n            AUTH_REPLY,\n            compat_struct_pack('!BBBB', SOCKS5_VERSION, Socks5Error.ERR_SUCCESS, 0, Socks5AddressType.ATYP_IPV4),\n            bound_addr,\n            compat_struct_pack('!H', 4242),\n        ])\n\n        result = socks._setup_socks5(('1.2.3.4', 1080))\n\n        assert result == (bound_addr, 4242)\n        close_mock.assert_not_called()\n        # auth packet + connect packet\n        assert send_mock.call_count == 2\n        auth_packet, connect_packet = [c.args[0] for c in send_mock.call_args_list]\n        assert auth_packet == compat_struct_pack('!BBB', SOCKS5_VERSION, 1, Socks5Auth.AUTH_NONE)\n        assert connect_packet == (\n            compat_struct_pack('!BBB', SOCKS5_VERSION, Socks5Command.CMD_CONNECT, 0)\n            + compat_struct_pack('!B', Socks5AddressType.ATYP_IPV4)\n            + socket.inet_aton('1.2.3.4')\n            + compat_struct_pack('!H', 1080)\n        )\n        assert [c.args[0] for c in recv_mock.call_args_list] == [2, 4, 4, 2]\n```\n\nWait \u2014 `sendall` mock: `send_mock.call_args_list` \u2014 each call `sendall(packet)` \u2192 args[0] = packet. Good. But careful: `mock.Mock()` called with positional arg \u2192 `c.args[0]` works in Python 3.8+ (`c.args` exists since 3.8). To be safe for older Pythons, use `c[0][0]`. youtube_dl is old (Python 2.7/3.x era) but tests run on modern pytest presumably. I'll use `call_args_list[i].args[0]`... hmm, `call.args` was added in Python 3.8. To be maximally compatible, use `c[0][0]`. I'll use indexing form to be safe.\n\nAlso note: auth packet has 3 bytes: version, nmethods, method \u2192 `compat_struct_pack('!BBB', SOCKS5_VERSION, 1, Socks5Auth.AUTH_NONE)`. From `_socks5_auth`: packet = pack('!B', 5) + pack('!B', 1) + pack('!1B', AUTH_NONE) \u2192 b'\\x05\\x01\\x00'. Yes.\n\nTest 2:\n\n```python\n    def test_connect_remote_dns_domain_reply(self, socks, monkeypatch):\n        bound_addr = b'example.org'\n        recv_mock, send_mock, close_mock = _patch_io(monkeypatch, socks, [\n            AUTH_REPLY,\n            compat_struct_pack('!BBBB', SOCKS5_VERSION, Socks5Error.ERR_SUCCESS, 0, Socks5AddressType.ATYP_DOMAINNAME),\n            compat_struct_pack('!B', len(bound_addr)),\n            bound_addr,\n            compat_struct_pack('!H', 1080),\n        ])\n\n        result = socks._setup_socks5(('example.com', 1080))\n\n        assert result == (bound_addr, 1080)\n        _, connect_packet = [c[0][0] for c in send_mock.call_args_list]  # careful unpacking\n```\n\nHmm, `[c[0][0] for c in ...]` gives list of packets; unpack: `auth_packet, connect_packet = ...`. Fine.\n\n```python\n        assert connect_packet == (\n            compat_struct_pack('!BBB', SOCKS5_VERSION, Socks5Command.CMD_CONNECT, 0)\n            + compat_struct_pack('!B', Socks5AddressType.ATYP_DOMAINNAME)\n            + compat_struct_pack('!B', len(b'example.com'))\n            + b'example.com'\n            + compat_struct_pack('!H', 1080)\n        )\n        assert [c[0][0] for c in recv_mock.call_args_list] == [2, 4, 1, len(bound_addr), 2]\n```\n\nNote: default setproxy rdns=True \u2192 remote_dns True \u2192 ipaddr None. Good.\n\nTest 3 (IPv6 reply):\n\n```python\n    def test_connect_ipv6_reply(self, socks, monkeypatch):\n        bound_addr = compat_struct_pack('!8H', 0x2001, 0x0DB8, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n        recv_mock, send_mock, close_mock = _patch_io(monkeypatch, socks, [\n            AUTH_REPLY,\n            compat_struct_pack('!BBBB', SOCKS5_VERSION, Socks5Error.ERR_SUCCESS, 0, Socks5AddressType.ATYP_IPV6),\n            bound_addr,\n            compat_struct_pack('!H', 4242),\n        ])\n\n        result = socks._setup_socks5(('1.2.3.4', 4242))\n\n        assert result == (bound_addr, 4242)\n        assert [c[0][0] for c in recv_mock.call_args_list] == [2, 4, 16, 2]\n```\n\nTest 4 (error):\n\n```python\n    def test_error_status_closes_socket_and_raises(self, socks, monkeypatch):\n        recv_mock, send_mock, close_mock = _patch_io(monkeypatch, socks, [\n            AUTH_REPLY,\n            compat_struct_pack('!BBBB', SOCKS5_VERSION, Socks5Error.ERR_GENERAL_FAILURE, 0, Socks5AddressType.ATYP_IPV4),\n        ])\n\n        with pytest.raises(Socks5Error) as excinfo:\n            socks._setup_socks5(('1.2.3.4', 1080))\n\n        assert excinfo.value.errno == Socks5Error.ERR_GENERAL_FAILURE\n        assert excinfo.value.strerror == 'general SOCKS server failure'\n        close_mock.assert_called_once_with()\n        # No attempt to read the bound address after the failure.\n        assert [c[0][0] for c in recv_mock.call_args_list] == [2, 4]\n```\n\nTest 5 (invalid version):\n\n```python\n    def test_invalid_response_version_closes_socket_and_raises(self, socks, monkeypatch):\n        recv_mock, send_mock, close_mock = _patch_io(monkeypatch, socks, [\n            AUTH_REPLY,\n            compat_struct_pack('!BBBB', SOCKS5_VERSION - 1, Socks5Error.ERR_SUCCESS, 0, Socks5AddressType.ATYP_IPV4),\n        ])\n\n        with pytest.raises(InvalidVersionError):\n            socks._setup_socks5(('1.2.3.4', 1080))\n\n        close_mock.assert_called_once_with()\n```\n\nTest 6 (rdns=False local resolution):\n\n```python\n    def test_connect_local_dns_resolution(self, socks, monkeypatch):\n        monkeypatch.setattr(socket, 'gethostbyname', lambda host: '9.9.9.9')\n        bound_addr = compat_struct_pack('!BBBB', 1, 2, 3, 4)\n        recv_mock, send_mock, close_mock = _patch_io(monkeypatch, socks, [\n            AUTH_REPLY,\n            compat_struct_pack('!BBBB', SOCKS5_VERSION, Socks5Error.ERR_SUCCESS, 0, Socks5AddressType.ATYP_IPV4),\n            bound_addr,\n            compat_struct_pack('!H', 8080),\n        ])\n\n        result = socks._setup_socks5(('example.com', 8080))\n\n        assert result == (bound_addr, 8080)\n        _, connect_packet = [c[0][0] for c in send_mock.call_args_list]\n        assert connect_packet[3:4] == compat_struct_pack('!B', Socks5AddressType.ATYP_IPV4)\n        assert connect_packet[4:8] == socket.inet_aton('9.9.9.9')\n```\n\nWait \u2014 setproxy default rdns=True; I need rdns=False here. My fixture creates the socket without setproxy... Let me restructure: fixture creates raw socket; each test calls `socks.setproxy(...)` with desired rdns. Or the helper `_patch_io` also does setproxy with params. Let me make a helper:\n\n```python\ndef _prepare(monkeypatch, sock, recv_chunks, rdns=True):\n    sock.setproxy(ProxyType.SOCKS5, '127.0.0.1', 1080, rdns=rdns)\n    ...\n```\n\nGood.\n\nTest 7 (unknown atype):\n\n```python\n    def test_unknown_reply_address_type_falls_through(self, socks, monkeypatch):\n        recv_mock, send_mock, close_mock = _patch_io(monkeypatch, socks, [\n            AUTH_REPLY,\n            compat_struct_pack('!BBBB', SOCKS5_VERSION, Socks5Error.ERR_SUCCESS, 0, 0),  # atype 0: unknown\n            compat_struct_pack('!H', 4242),\n        ])\n\n        result = socks._setup_socks5(('1.2.3.4', 4242))\n\n        assert result == ('1.2.3.4', 4242)\n        assert [c[0][0] for c in recv_mock.call_args_list] == [2, 4, 2]\n```\n\nNow \u2014 one thing to double check: `socks.setproxy(ProxyType.SOCKS5, '127.0.0.1', 1080, rdns=rdns)` \u2014 signature: `setproxy(self, proxytype, addr, port, rdns=True, username=None, password=None)`. Good.\n\nAnother check: `_resolve_address('1.2.3.4', None, use_remote_dns=True)` \u2192 `socket.inet_aton('1.2.3.4')` \u2192 b'\\x01\\x02\\x03\\x04'. Good, no network.\n\nFor 'example.com' with rdns=True: inet_aton raises socket.error (actually `socket.error` is OSError; inet_aton raises `OSError: illegal IP address string passed to inet_aton`). Caught \u2192 returns None (default) since remote_dns True. Good, no network.\n\nFor 'example.com' with rdns=False: gethostbyname mocked. Good.\n\nNow \u2014 potential issue: `sockssocket()` creation. `sockssocket.__init__(self, *args, **kwargs)` \u2192 sets `_proxy = None` \u2192 `super().__init__()` \u2192 creates AF_INET/SOCK_STREAM socket. Fine.\n\nAnother potential issue: when `_setup_socks5` raises in test 4/5, it calls `self.close()` (the mock) \u2014 real socket still open \u2014 fixture closes it. Good.\n\nAlso \u2014 `monkeypatch.setattr(sock, 'recv', recv_mock)`: `sock.recv` exists as class attribute \u2192 raising=True OK.\n\nNow, about `AUTH_REPLY` module-level constant: `compat_struct_pack('!BB', SOCKS5_VERSION, Socks5Auth.AUTH_NONE)`. Socks5Auth.AUTH_NONE \u2014 I'm confident it exists (referenced in `_socks5_auth`). Value presumably 0. Using the symbol is robust.\n\nOne more consideration: the user said \"Write as little top-level code as possible\" \u2014 module-level constants like AUTH_REPLY are fine (they're just constants, not executable test logic). Maybe keep them minimal. I'll define AUTH_REPLY as a module-level constant \u2014 acceptable. Or compute inline in helper. I'll keep one small constant.\n\nAlso \"do not include any top-level code calling into pytest.main\" \u2014 fine.\n\nShould tests be in a class or plain functions? Plain functions are fine. I'll use plain functions with a shared fixture.\n\nLet me also think about whether `socks` fixture name conflicts with the imported module `youtube_dl.socks`... I'm importing specific names, not the module, so no conflict. But I do `import socket` \u2014 and fixture named `socks` \u2014 no conflict.\n\nWait, actually \u2014 I should double-check `Socks5Error.ERR_GENERAL_FAILURE` exists: yes, from fetched source: `ERR_GENERAL_FAILURE = 1`. And `ERR_SUCCESS = 0` is on ProxyError (inherited). I'll use `Socks5Error.ERR_SUCCESS` \u2014 inherited from ProxyError, accessible. Good.\n\nNow, `excinfo.value.errno` \u2014 Socks5Error(1) \u2192 ProxyError.__init__(code=1, msg=None) \u2192 msg = CODES.get(1) \u2192 'general SOCKS server failure' \u2192 super().__init__(1, 'general SOCKS server failure') \u2192 OSError.__new__ with 2 args \u2192 .errno = 1, .strerror = 'general SOCKS server failure'. \n\nHmm wait, one subtlety: `ProxyError.__init__(self, code=None, msg=None)` \u2014 from fetched source:\n\n```python\ndef __init__(self, code=None, msg=None):\n    if code is not None and msg is None:\n        msg = self.CODES.get(code) or 'unknown error'\n    super(ProxyError, self).__init__(code, msg)\n```\n\nSo Socks5Error(1) \u2192 args (1, 'general SOCKS server failure'). errno=1. Good.\n\nFor InvalidVersionError \u2014 I don't know its constructor; I'll just assert the type is raised and close was called. Safe.\n\nNow let me also double check `_recv_bytes(4)` unpack format `'!4B'` \u2014 returns 4 ints. version=5, status=0, reserved=0, atype=1. Good.\n\nAnd `compat_struct_unpack('!H', self.recvall(2))[0]` \u2192 port int.\n\nEdge: in test 2, `recvall(11)` \u2192 `self.recv(11)` \u2192 returns b'example.org' (11 bytes) \u2192 loop ends. Good.\n\nNow, one concern about `mock.Mock(side_effect=[...])`: if the code calls recv more times than provided, StopIteration \u2192 test fails loudly. Good.\n\nLet me also verify the request packet for test 1 more carefully:\n\n`_setup_socks5(('1.2.3.4', 1080))`:\n- destaddr='1.2.3.4', port=1080\n- ipaddr = inet_aton('1.2.3.4') = b'\\x01\\x02\\x03\\x04'\n- _socks5_auth: sendall(b'\\x05\\x01\\x00'); recv 2 \u2192 (5, 0); check version OK; method=0 \u2260 AUTH_NO_ACCEPTABLE(255?), not USER_PASS \u2192 done.\n- packet = pack('!BBB', 5, 1, 0) = b'\\x05\\x01\\x00'\n- ipaddr not None \u2192 packet += pack('!B', 1) + b'\\x01\\x02\\x03\\x04' \u2192 b'\\x05\\x01\\x00\\x01\\x01\\x02\\x03\\x04'\n- packet += pack('!H', 1080) = b'\\x04\\x38' \u2192 full: b'\\x05\\x01\\x00\\x01\\x01\\x02\\x03\\x04\\x04\\x38'\n- sendall(packet) \u2014 2nd send call\n- _recv_bytes(4) \u2192 recv(4) \u2192 (5, 0, 0, 1)\n- _check_response_version(5, 5) OK\n- status == 0 \u2192 no error\n- atype == 1 \u2192 destaddr = recvall(4) \u2192 recv(4) \u2192 b'\\x0a\\x0b\\x0c\\x0d'\n- destport = unpack('!H', recvall(2))[0] \u2192 recv(2) \u2192 b'\\x10\\x92' \u2192 4242\n- return (b'\\x0a\\x0b\\x0c\\x0d', 4242)\n\n\u2713.\n\nTest 2: `_setup_socks5(('example.com', 1080))` with rdns=True:\n- inet_aton('example.com') raises \u2192 remote_dns True \u2192 ipaddr=None\n- auth as before\n- packet = b'\\x05\\x01\\x00' + b'\\x03' + _len_and_data(b'example.com') = b'\\x0b' + b'example.com' + b'\\x04\\x38'\n- recv(4) \u2192 (5,0,0,3)\n- atype==3 \u2192 alen = ord(recv(1)) \u2192 recv(1) \u2192 b'\\x0b' \u2192 11\n- destaddr = recvall(11) \u2192 recv(11) \u2192 b'example.org'\n- destport = recv(2) \u2192 1080\n- return (b'example.org', 1080)\n\n\u2713.\n\nNow, the recv call sizes for test 2: [2, 4, 1, 11, 2]. \u2713.\n\nTest 3: atype=4 \u2192 recvall(16) \u2192 recv(16). Sizes [2,4,16,2]. \u2713.\n\nTest 7: atype=0 \u2192 no addr recv; sizes [2,4,2]; destaddr stays '1.2.3.4' (string, since ipaddr was not None so line 226 didn't reassign). Return ('1.2.3.4', 4242). \u2713.\n\nNow, about coverage of line 246 `alen = compat_ord(self.recv(1))` \u2014 test 2. \u2713. Line 244 \u2014 tests 1, 3, 6. Line 249 \u2014 test 3. Line 250 \u2014 all success tests. Line 252 \u2014 all success tests.\n\nLines 226-228 \u2014 test 2. Line 230 \u2014 tests 1, 3, 6, 7. Lines 239-241 \u2014 test 4. Line 237 \u2014 all.\n\nGreat, full coverage of 217-252 including all branches.\n\nNow, cleanup considerations:\n- Fixture closes real socket via `socket.socket.close(sock)`.\n- monkeypatch restores recv/sendall/close and gethostbyname.\n- No global state modified otherwise.\n\nOne more thought: should I worry that `sockssocket()` might emit a ResourceWarning if pytest is configured with `-W error`? Our fixture closes it, so fine.\n\nAlso \u2014 the user wants \"assertions that verify any applicable postconditions\" \u2014 I have result assertions, packet assertions, close assertions, recv-size assertions. Good.\n\nLet me also consider: should the fixture use `setproxy`? I moved setproxy into `_prepare`. The fixture just creates the socket. Fine.\n\nLet me now also handle a subtle thing: `monkeypatch.setattr(sock, 'close', close_mock)` \u2014 then in fixture teardown, `socket.socket.close(sock)` \u2014 direct class call, unaffected. \u2713.\n\nBut wait \u2014 there's an ordering issue: fixture teardown runs `socket.socket.close(sock)` \u2014 but what if monkeypatch teardown ran first and restored close? Doesn't matter, we call the class method directly. What if fixture teardown runs first (socks fixture set up after monkeypatch in parameter order)? Also fine \u2014 direct class call. \u2713.\n\nHmm, one more: in the fixture, after `yield sock`, if the test failed with the socket in a weird state \u2014 close is still fine.\n\nNow, imports \u2014 let me be careful about what exists in youtube_dl.socks:\n- sockssocket \u2713\n- ProxyType \u2713 (referenced in setproxy)\n- Socks5Auth \u2713 (referenced in _socks5_auth)\n- Socks5AddressType \u2713 (fetched)\n- Socks5Command \u2713 (fetched)\n- Socks4Command \u2713 (fetched) \u2014 not needed directly\n- Socks5Error \u2713 (fetched)\n- InvalidVersionError \u2014 referenced in _check_response_version; defined in socks.py presumably. I'll import it. Risk: if it's named differently... The fetched `_check_response_version` code says `raise InvalidVersionError(expected_version, got_version)` \u2014 so the name is `InvalidVersionError` and it's resolvable in socks.py's namespace. Import should work.\n- SOCKS5_VERSION \u2713 (fetched)\n\nFrom youtube_dl.compat: compat_struct_pack \u2713 (used in the snippet's import line).\n\nLet me write the final test file.\n\n```python\nimport socket\nfrom unittest import mock\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom youtube_dl.compat import compat_struct_pack\nfrom youtube_dl.socks import (\n    SOCKS5_VERSION,\n    InvalidVersionError,\n    ProxyType,\n    Socks5AddressType,\n    Socks5Auth,\n    Socks5Command,\n    Socks5Error,\n    sockssocket,\n)\n\nAUTH_REPLY = compat_struct_pack('!BB', SOCKS5_VERSION, Socks5Auth.AUTH_NONE)\n\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef socks():\n    sock = sockssocket()\n    yield sock\n    # Close the real underlying socket, bypassing any instance-level mock\n    # of `close`, so no file descriptor leaks between tests.\n    socket.socket.close(sock)\n\n\ndef _prepare(monkeypatch, sock, recv_chunks, rdns=True):\n    \"\"\"Wire up a proxy config and fake transport I/O on a sockssocket.\"\"\"\n    sock.setproxy(ProxyType.SOCKS5, '127.0.0.1', 1080, rdns=rdns)\n    recv_mock = mock.Mock(side_effect=list(recv_chunks))\n    send_mock = mock.Mock()\n    close_mock = mock.Mock()\n    monkeypatch.setattr(sock, 'recv', recv_mock)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(sock, 'sendall', send_mock)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(sock, 'close', close_mock)\n    return recv_mock, send_mock, close_mock\n\n\ndef _sent_packets(send_mock):\n    return [call[0][0] for call in send_mock.call_args_list]\n\n\ndef _recv_sizes(recv_mock):\n    return [call[0][0] for call in recv_mock.call_args_list]\n\n\ndef test_socks5_ip_destination_ipv4_reply(socks, monkeypatch):\n    bound_addr = compat_struct_pack('!BBBB', 10, 11, 12, 13)\n    recv_mock, send_mock, close_mock = _prepare(monkeypatch, socks, [\n        AUTH_REPLY,\n        compat_struct_pack('!BBBB', SOCKS5_VERSION, Socks5Error.ERR_SUCCESS, 0, Socks5AddressType.ATYP_IPV4),\n        bound_addr,\n        compat_struct_pack('!H', 4242),\n    ])\n\n    result = socks._setup_socks5(('1.2.3.4', 1080))\n\n    assert result == (bound_addr, 4242)\n    close_mock.assert_not_called()\n    auth_packet, connect_packet = _sent_packets(send_mock)\n    assert auth_packet == compat_struct_pack('!BBB', SOCKS5_VERSION, 1, Socks5Auth.AUTH_NONE)\n    assert connect_packet == (\n        compat_struct_pack('!BBB', SOCKS5_VERSION, Socks5Command.CMD_CONNECT, 0)\n        + compat_struct_pack('!B', Socks5AddressType.ATYP_IPV4)\n        + socket.inet_aton('1.2.3.4')\n        + compat_struct_pack('!H', 1080)\n    )\n    assert _recv_sizes(recv_mock) == [2, 4, 4, 2]\n```\n\netc.\n\nWait \u2014 one thing about `_sent_packets` unpacking `auth_packet, connect_packet = _sent_packets(send_mock)` \u2014 requires exactly 2 packets. \u2713.\n\nTest 2:\n\n```python\ndef test_socks5_remote_dns_domain_destination_and_reply(socks, monkeypatch):\n    bound_addr = b'example.org'\n    recv_mock, send_mock, close_mock = _prepare(monkeypatch, socks, [\n        AUTH_REPLY,\n        compat_struct_pack('!BBBB', SOCKS5_VERSION, Socks5Error.ERR_SUCCESS, 0, Socks5AddressType.ATYP_DOMAINNAME),\n        compat_struct_pack('!B', len(bound_addr)),\n        bound_addr,\n        compat_struct_pack('!H', 1080),\n    ])\n\n    result = socks._setup_socks5(('example.com', 1080))\n\n    assert result == (bound_addr, 1080)\n    close_mock.assert_not_called()\n    _, connect_packet = _sent_packets(send_mock)\n    assert connect_packet == (\n        compat_struct_pack('!BBB', SOCKS5_VERSION, Socks5Command.CMD_CONNECT, 0)\n        + compat_struct_pack('!B', Socks5AddressType.ATYP_DOMAINNAME)\n        + compat_struct_pack('!B', len(b'example.com'))\n        + b'example.com'\n        + compat_struct_pack('!H', 1080)\n    )\n    assert _recv_sizes(recv_mock) == [2, 4, 1, len(bound_addr), 2]\n```\n\nTest 3:\n\n```python\ndef test_socks5_ipv6_reply_address(socks, monkeypatch):\n    bound_addr = compat_struct_pack('!8H', 0x2001, 0x0DB8, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1)\n    recv_mock, send_mock, close_mock = _prepare(monkeypatch, socks, [\n        AUTH_REPLY,\n        compat_struct_pack('!BBBB', SOCKS5_VERSION, Socks5Error.ERR_SUCCESS, 0, Socks5AddressType.ATYP_IPV6),\n        bound_addr,\n        compat_struct_pack('!H', 4242),\n    ])\n\n    result = socks._setup_socks5(('1.2.3.4', 4242))\n\n    assert result == (bound_addr, 4242)\n    assert _recv_sizes(recv_mock) == [2, 4, 16, 2]\n    close_mock.assert_not_called()\n```\n\nTest 4:\n\n```python\ndef test_socks5_error_status_closes_socket_and_raises(socks, monkeyp