ot None:\n    ...\noriginal, local = self.get_original()\n```\n\n`get_original`:\n\n```python\ndef get_original(self):\n    target = self.getter()\n    name = self.attribute\n\n    original = DEFAULT\n    local = False\n\n    try:\n        original = target.__dict__[name]\n    except (AttributeError, KeyError):\n        original = getattr(target, name, DEFAULT)\n    else:\n        local = True\n    ...\n```\n\nAnd in `__exit__`:\n\n```python\nif not self.is_local and self.temp_original is not DEFAULT:\n    delattr(self.target, self.attribute)\n```\n\nHmm, `is_local` is set in `__enter__` based on whether the attribute was in `target.__dict__`. Since `close` is not in the instance `__dict__` initially, `is_local` False, and on exit it does `delattr(self.target, self.attribute)` \u2014 which deletes the instance attribute we set (the mock), restoring access to the class method. \n\nBut careful: if the code under test calls `self.close()` and our mock wraps it, the real close runs \u2014 good, socket actually closed.\n\nAlternatively, avoid patch and just check `s._closed` after the exception. Hmm, `_closed` \u2014 in Python 3, `socket.socket.__init__` sets `self._closed = False`. And `close()` sets `self._closed = True`. This is CPython implementation detail but stable. I'd rather use the wraps-mock for explicit call assertion. Both. Let me use mock.patch.object with wraps.\n\nActually, there's a subtle issue with `mock.patch.object(s, 'close', wraps=s.close)`: `wraps` with a bound method \u2014 calling the mock calls the bound method with the given args. `self.close()` \u2192 mock called with no args \u2192 calls bound `s.close()`. Good.\n\nNow, also need to make sure `sendall` mock: after `close()` is called in error path, `sendall` isn't called again, fine.\n\nOne more consideration: `compat_struct_unpack('!2B', data)` requires data length exactly 2. Our recvall mock returns exactly 2 bytes. Good.\n\nLet me now write the expected packets:\n\nScenario A (no auth):\n